            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I managed to reproduce it with `go_in` too.\n\nSince the keymaps _map_ directly to the functions, i will write it in lua (which i tested too):\n```lua\nlocal m = require('mini.files')                                                                                                                                                                                  \nm.open()                                                                                                                                                                                                         \nm.go_in()                                                                                                                                                                                                        \nm.close()                                                                                                                                                                                                        \nm.open()                                                                                                                                                                                                         \nvim.api.nvim_feedkeys(':q!', 'n', true)                                                                                                                                                                      \nm.open()\n```\n\nHere is the one liner:\n```lua\n:lua local m = require('mini.files'); m.open(); m.go_in(); m.close(); m.open(); vim.api.nvim_feedkeys(':q!', 'n', true); m.open();\n```\n\nSeems to me is the combination of `go_in` and `close`, which go_in_plus seems (to me) to also do.",
